   "kind": 1,
   "value": "*Printed output of the code cell above (evaluated from exactly the saved source in kernel K1 via Get; not a native notebook output).*\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| check | result |\n| S2 double-pole coefficients c2 (s = 1, 2) at P and P'; prediction lambda2 (lambda1 + 1) | {{8, 8}, {8, 8}, 8} |\n| S2 double poles are scalars (P, P') | {{True, True}, {True, True}} |\n| S3 triple poles S3c[s,3] are exactly 0 (s = 1, 2) at P, P' | {{True, True}, {True, True}} |\n| S2(inf) = e2(zeta), S3(inf) = e3(zeta) at P and P' (exact) | {True, True, True, True} |\n| the six non-scalar Laurent data at P: is the operator a scalar? H1, H2, S3c[1,1], S3c[1,2], S3c[2,1], S3c[2,2] | {False, False, False, False, False, False} |\n| c_Lambda and exact operator identity S3c[s,1] = c S3c[s,2](H_s - S3c[s,2]): {{c, holds} site 1, site 2} at P, then P' | {{{1, False}, {1, False}}, {{1, False}, {1, False}}} |\n| same at P for theta2 = -3/5, 1/4, 4/3, -1 (alternative Laurent samples) | {{{1, False}, {1, False}}, {{1, False}, {1, False}}, {{1, False}, {1, False}}, {{1, False}, {1, False}}} |\n| candidate c_Lambda in {1/4, 1/3, 1/5, 1/6, 1/8, 1/(l1+1), 1/\\|Lambda\\|}: which holds exactly at P (site 1, site 2) | {{1/4, False, False}, {1/3, False, False}, {1/5, False, False}, {1/6, False, False}, {1/8, False, False}, {1/4, False, False}, {1/5, False, False}} |",
